    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If cleaning the data types doesn't solve it, then you might be running into a very specific, rare issue, which is almost certainly related to how Excel handles object references or memory when the file gets large or complex. Sometimes, a hidden \"glitch\" is just Excel getting overwhelmed by too many active objects, which is especially true if you have other complex VBA code running in the background.\n\nTo test this, try adding a `Application.ScreenUpdating = False` at the very beginning of your `MonthlyRecon` sub, and then setting it back to `True` before the end. This stops Excel from trying to redraw the screen for every single cell change, which can sometimes prevent these intermittent, seemingly random crashes. This is a common tactic for stabilizing macros that run on large datasets.",
